<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bergedorf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bergedorf is a borough in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 1420 Hamburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> took the town and its castle from the dukes of Saxe-Lauenburg and ruled it jointly, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vierlande">Vierlande</a>, for more than four centuries. In 1868 Hamburg bought out Lübeck's share, and Bergedorf has been part of Hamburg ever since.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergedorf.jpg" alt="Bergedorf" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IqRS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bergedorfer_Hafen.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bergedorf lies in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, on the river Bille, a right tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. The borough covers 154.8 square kilometres and comprises the districts of Allermöhe, Altengamme, Bergedorf, Billwerder, Curslack, Kirchwerder, Lohbrügge, Moorfleet, Neuallermöhe, Neuengamme, Ochsenwerder, Reitbrook, Spadenland and Tatenberg. It is the largest of Hamburg's seven boroughs and is nicknamed the Garden of Hamburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bergedorf received town privileges in 1275, when it belonged to the younger Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>; it passed to Saxe-Lauenburg in 1296. In 1321 Duke Eric conceded Bergedorf with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vierlande">Vierlande</a> to his brother John II, whose share became known as Saxe-Bergedorf-Mölln. In 1370 John's successor Eric III pawned the lordship of Bergedorf, the Vierlande, half the Saxon Wood and Geesthacht to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> for 16,262.5 Lübeck marks. The pledge included much of the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and Lübeck, giving safe passage for freight between the cities.</p><p>Eric IV of Saxe-Ratzeburg-Lauenburg seized the pawned areas by force in 1401 without repayment. In 1420 his son Eric V attacked Prince-Elector Frederick I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> opened a second front, conquering Bergedorf, Riepenburg castle and the Esslingen river toll station within weeks. By the Peace of Perleberg of 23 August 1420 the territories were irrevocably ceded to the two cities.</p><p>The cities ruled the area as a bi-urban condominium governed by bailiffs, alternately from each city, with terms of four years, six from 1446 and life terms from 1620. In 1542 the bailiff Ditmar Koel introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> there. The area was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">First French Empire</a> between 1811 and 1813. The first railway in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> opened between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and Bergedorf in 1842, and the condominium issued its own postage stamps between 1861 and 1867. On 1 January 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> sold its share to Hamburg for 200,000 Prussian thaler, and on 1 April 1938 Bergedorf became a borough of Hamburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The borough has a station for suburban, local and long-distance trains, and its notable buildings include the church of St. Petri and Pauli, Schloss Bergedorf and the Hamburg-Bergedorf Observatory. Its stamps of the condominium are of note to philatelists, since with a population of 12,468 in 1863 it was by far the smallest German territory to issue them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bergedorf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes were often in debt and pledged lands, including the Vierlande and <b>Bergedorf</b>, to Lübeck and Hamburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-lauenburg">Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg</a></li></ul></section>
